    "text": "Bicycle gears are a mechanical system designed to optimize the relationship between the rider’s pedaling speed and the speed of the wheels. By changing gears, a rider can adjust the **mechanical advantage** of the bike, making it easier to climb hills, accelerate from a stop, or maintain high speeds on flat terrain.\n\n### How Bicycle Gears Work\n\n#### 1. Core Components\n- **Chainrings (Front Gears):** Attached to the crankset (where the pedals are). Most modern bikes have 1–3 chainrings.\n- **Cassette (Rear Gears):** A stack of sprockets on the rear wheel hub. Typically has 8–12 cogs.\n- **Chain:** Connects the front chainring to the rear cog.\n- **Derailleurs:** Mechanical arms that move the chain laterally to engage different gears.\n  - **Front Derailleur:** Moves the chain between front chainrings.\n  - **Rear Derailleur:** Moves the chain between rear cogs.\n\n#### 2. The Gear Ratio\nThe **gear ratio** is calculated as:\n\\[\n\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Teeth on Front Chain",
